<h1>Monitoring Live Strategies Statistically: Drift, Divergence, and Drawdown</h1>
<div class="why"><strong>Why this matters here:</strong> the July 6 batch added three
watchdogs to the arena — PSI drift checks (<code>drift.py</code>), the live-vs-backtest
z-test (<code>strategy_arena.divergence_stats</code>), and the drawdown circuit breaker.
They look unrelated, but they are one idea applied at three points in the pipeline:
<em>is the live process still consistent with the reference I validated?</em></div>

<h2 id="s1">1 · One idea, three checkpoints</h2>
<table>
<tr><th>Checkpoint</th><th>Question</th><th>Reference</th><th>Tool in this repo</th></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Inputs</strong></td><td>Do the features the model sees still look like training data?</td><td>Decile histogram of each training feature</td><td>PSI — <code>drift.py</code>, <code>predict-next --check_drift</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Outputs</strong></td><td>Are live returns consistent with what the backtest promised?</td><td>Backtest daily-return mean/std/n ("fingerprint")</td><td>One-sided z-test — <code>divergence_stats</code>, flagged in the report</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Path</strong></td><td>Has the equity path lost more than I can tolerate, regardless of statistics?</td><td>Running peak since the last human sign-off</td><td>Drawdown breaker — PAUSED status, <code>arena-run resume</code></td></tr>
</table>
<p>Inputs can drift while outputs still look fine (the model coasts on a regime it
half-recognizes). Outputs can diverge while inputs look normal (overfitting: same
inputs, worse decisions). And a path can breach risk tolerance even when nothing is
statistically "wrong" — a 2σ-consistent losing streak still loses money. Three
different failure surfaces, three watchdogs. Only the path check acts autonomously
(pausing), and even it never un-pauses itself — detection is automated, judgment is human.</p>

<h2 id="s2">2 · The math, minimally</h2>
<p><span class="path">drift.py — Population Stability Index</span></p>
<pre><code>PSI = Σ (live%ᵢ − ref%ᵢ) · ln(live%ᵢ / ref%ᵢ)   <span class="cm"># over 10 decile bins</span>
<span class="cm"># ref% is 1/10 per bin BY CONSTRUCTION — the edges are the training data's
# own quantiles. Convention: &lt;0.1 fine · 0.1–0.25 moderate · &gt;0.25 flagged.</span></code></pre>
<p><span class="path">strategy_arena.divergence_stats — one-sided z-test</span></p>
<pre><code>z = (live_mean − bt_mean) / (bt_std / √n_live)
<span class="cm"># Flag when z &lt; −2 after ≥20 live days. One-sided: only UNDERperformance
# signals overfitting; outperformance is luck, not an alarm.
# NOT a same-period shadow backtest — live and backtest share one
# deterministic code path here, so that comparison is zero by construction
# (lesson 0035 covers the shared-path design).</span></code></pre>
<p>Both formulas ask "how far is the live sample from the reference, in units of
the reference's own noise?" — PSI in distribution space, z in mean space.</p>

<h2 id="s3">3 · Calibration: respect the sampling noise</h2>
<p>The regime classifier (same batch) taught this the hard way. The plan set the
trending/mean-reverting threshold at |lag-1 autocorrelation| &gt; 0.05. But the
standard error of an autocorrelation estimate over 60 days is ≈ 1/√60 ≈ 0.13 —
so <em>pure white noise</em> lands outside ±0.05 most of the time, and "neutral"
would have been a rare label for markets with no signal at all. The shipped
threshold is 0.15 (~1 SE). The general rule: <strong>a monitoring threshold
tighter than the estimator's own sampling noise doesn't detect anomalies —
it manufactures them.</strong> That's also why divergence needs
<code>min_live_days</code> before it evaluates: with n=3, the SE is enormous and
every strategy would flag.</p>

<h2 id="s4">4 · Often confused with</h2>
<table>
<tr><th></th><th>Input drift (PSI)</th><th>Output divergence (z)</th><th>Drawdown breach</th></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Detects</strong></td><td>World changed under the model</td><td>Model's edge wasn't real (overfit)</td><td>Loss exceeded tolerance</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Can fire alone?</strong></td><td>Yes — outputs may still be fine</td><td>Yes — inputs may look normal</td><td>Yes — no statistical anomaly needed</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Action</strong></td><td>Flag; consider retraining</td><td>Flag; human reviews the strategy</td><td>Auto-pause; human resume only</td></tr>
</table>
<p>Also distinct: lesson 0035's <em>train/serve skew</em> is a pipeline bug (your code
feeds different inputs); PSI drift is the world legitimately changing while your
pipeline is correct. Same symptom in the report, different fix.</p>

<h2 id="s5">5 · Practice</h2>
<p>Score: <span id="score">0 / 0</span></p>

<div class="question" data-question>
  <p><strong>Q1.</strong> Live Sharpe looks fine, but <code>predict-next --check_drift</code>
  flags <code>vol_10</code> at PSI 0.4. What does that combination tell you?</p>
  <ul class="options">
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="PSI measures inputs, not returns — it cannot be 'overridden' by good performance.">Nothing — good live returns mean the drift flag is a false positive</li>
    <li data-correct="true" data-why="Exactly: the model is operating outside its training regime even though results haven't degraded YET. That's the point of monitoring inputs separately — you get the warning before the loss.">The model is running on inputs it wasn't trained for; performance may follow later</li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="The breaker watches the equity path, not feature distributions — separate watchdog entirely.">The circuit breaker should have paused it already</li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="Backwards — the fingerprint is for the OUTPUT divergence test. PSI's reference is the feature histograms.">The backtest fingerprint needs re-recording</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="feedback" hidden></p>
</div>

<div class="question" data-question>
  <p><strong>Q2.</strong> Why is the divergence test one-sided (only z &lt; −2 flags)?</p>
  <ul class="options">
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="Two-sided would be symmetric, but the point is what the flag MEANS, not statistical convention.">Because two-sided tests need twice the sample size</li>
    <li data-correct="true" data-why="Right — the alert exists to catch overfitting, and overfitting shows up as live UNDERperformance vs. the backtest. Outperformance is luck; alarming on it would train you to ignore the flag.">Only underperformance is evidence of overfitting; outperformance is just luck</li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="The breaker handles losses in dollar terms; the z-test is about statistical consistency, and it deliberately doesn't pause anything.">Because gains can't trip the drawdown breaker anyway</li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="The sign convention is a design decision, not a limitation of the z statistic.">z-scores are only defined for negative deviations</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="feedback" hidden></p>
</div>

<div class="question" data-question>
  <p><strong>Q3.</strong> A strategy pauses at −21% drawdown. You resume it. Why does the
  arena reset the high-water mark to the resume point instead of keeping the old peak?</p>
  <ul class="options">
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="It's not about generosity — with the old peak, the very next tick re-evaluates ≤ −20% and re-pauses instantly, making resume meaningless.">To be generous and give the strategy a fresh start</li>
    <li data-correct="true" data-why="Correct. Equity is still ~21% below the old peak, so against that anchor the breaker re-trips on the next tick. Resume is a human vote of confidence — it gets full threshold headroom measured from here.">Against the old peak it would still be past the threshold and re-pause immediately</li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="The paused window is excluded from BACKFILL (since_date), which is a different mechanism from the drawdown anchor.">Because the paused window's equity rows were deleted</li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="Thresholds don't accumulate slack in this design — that option was explicitly rejected in the plan as hard to reason about.">Because the threshold widens by 5% after each resume</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="feedback" hidden></p>
</div>

<div class="question" data-question>
  <p><strong>Q4.</strong> Why did the regime classifier's neutral band move from ±0.05 to ±0.15?</p>
  <ul class="options">
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="Backtests weren't slow — the issue was statistical, not computational.">±0.05 made backtests too slow</li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="Opposite direction — the tight band made trending/mean_reverting far too COMMON, not rare.">±0.05 made trending days too rare</li>
    <li data-correct="true" data-why="Yes — SE of lag-1 autocorrelation at 60 samples is ≈1/√60≈0.13, so white noise exceeded ±0.05 most of the time and 'no signal' markets got gated as if they had structure. Thresholds must sit at or beyond the estimator's own noise floor.">White noise routinely exceeded ±0.05 by sampling error alone (SE ≈ 0.13 at n=60)</li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="high_vol has its own separate threshold (annualized vol), checked before autocorrelation at all.">To make high_vol easier to reach</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="feedback" hidden></p>
</div>

<div class="question" data-question>
  <p><strong>Q5.</strong> Which watchdog is allowed to act autonomously, and how far does its autonomy go?</p>
  <ul class="options">
    <li data-correct="true" data-why="Right — the breaker pauses on breach (stop digging now), but resume is exclusively `arena-run resume` by a human. Drift and divergence only ever flag.">The drawdown breaker may pause a strategy, but only a human can resume it</li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="Divergence deliberately never pauses — it's a diagnostic; the breaker is the actuator.">The divergence alert pauses strategies whose z drops below −2</li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="Drift flags and suggests retraining; automatic retraining was explicitly scoped out as a human decision.">The drift monitor retrains the model when PSI exceeds 0.25</li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="One of them does act: the breaker pauses. Pure-flag-everything was not the design.">None — all three only write report columns</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="feedback" hidden></p>
</div>

<script>
function shuffle(arr){
  for (let i = arr.length - 1; i > 0; i--){
    const j = Math.floor(Math.random() * (i + 1));
    [arr[i], arr[j]] = [arr[j], arr[i]];
  }
  return arr;
}
let answered = 0, right = 0;
const total = document.querySelectorAll('[data-question]').length;
document.querySelectorAll('[data-question]').forEach(q => {
  const list = q.querySelector('.options');
  shuffle([...list.children]).forEach(li => list.appendChild(li));
  const feedback = q.querySelector('.feedback');
  let done = false;
  list.querySelectorAll('li').forEach(li => {
    li.addEventListener('click', () => {
      const correct = li.dataset.correct === 'true';
      if (!done){ answered++; if (correct) right++; done = true; }
      li.classList.add(correct ? 'picked-right' : 'picked-wrong');
      feedback.hidden = false;
      feedback.textContent = (correct ? '✓ Correct. ' : '✗ Not quite. ') + li.dataset.why;
      feedback.style.color = correct ? 'var(--good)' : 'var(--bad)';
      document.getElementById('score').textContent = right + ' / ' + answered;
    });
  });
});
</script>
